      "my_answer": "Rapid growth, ulceration, bleeding, neurological symptoms (numbness, weakness, pain), lymphadenopathy, or features of perineural invasion (e.g., facial nerve palsy).",
      "rationale": "These suggest aggressive behavior, deeper invasion, or metastasis, which would require more urgent intervention and possibly imaging."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q6",
      "question": "What diagnostic test is indicated to confirm the diagnosis?",
      "my_answer": "Skin biopsy (punch or shave) for histopathological examination.",
      "rationale": "A tissue sample is required to definitively diagnose BCC and rule out other malignancies. Dermoscopy is suggestive but not diagnostic."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q7",
      "question": "What are the management options for this lesion?",
      "my_answer": "Surgical excision with clear margins (e.g., 4-6 mm for low-risk BCC) or Mohs micrographic surgery for tissue preservation and high cure rate. Alternatives include cryotherapy, topical agents (imiquimod, 5-FU), or radiation for non-surgical candidates.",
      "rationale": "Complete excision is standard for nodular BCC. Mohs is preferred for cosmetically sensitive areas like the nose and for high-risk features."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q8",
      "question": "What is the expected prognosis and follow-up?",
      "my_answer": "Excellent prognosis with complete excision; cure rate >95%. Follow-up with full skin examination every 6-12 months due to high risk of new skin cancers.",
      "rationale": "BCC rarely metastasizes but can recur locally. Patients with chronic sun damage are at risk for additional lesions, necessitating surveillance."
    }
  ],
  "additional_relevant_facts": [
    "The patient is a farmer with chronic sun damage, indicating high cumulative UV exposure, a major risk factor for BCC.",
    "The nose is a high-risk location for BCC recurrence and cosmetic concerns, favoring Mohs micrographic surgery.",
    "Absence of lymphadenopathy is reassuring but does not exclude local invasion; biopsy is essential.",
    "Dermoscopic findings of arborizing vessels and blue-gray nests are highly specific for BCC, but histopathology remains the gold standard.",
    "Consideration of immunosuppression or genetic syndromes (e.g., basal cell nevus syndrome) if multiple lesions or early onset, though not suggested here.",
